{
  "image_name": "0d337c905827e91d5f7c065911e87bb4.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes the DNA replication stress response and how stalled replication forks trigger checkpoint signaling. At the fork, the core replisome includes the MCM2–7 helicase with CDC45 and GINS, plus leading- and lagging-strand polymerases. When replication is perturbed, stretches of single-stranded DNA appear on the lagging side and are coated by RPA. That ssDNA recruits ATR through ATRIP, while a PCNA-like clamp and RFC-like loader help amplify the signal. The fork-protection proteins claspin, TIPIN, and TIMELESS are shown associated with the replisome, emphasizing replication fork stabilization.\n\nThe diagram links this stalled-fork state to checkpoint activation through ATR, which then signals mainly to CHK1. In parallel, double-strand breaks activate ATM, accompanied by γH2AX-marked chromatin, and ATM signals largely to CHK2. These checkpoint pathways converge on several outcomes: inhibition of late origin firing, promotion of DNA repair pathways, arrest of cell-cycle progression, and transcriptional responses such as p53 activation. Overall, the image presents replication stress as a coordinated process in which damaged or slowed forks are stabilized, sensed, and converted into broader nuclear responses that preserve genome integrity.",
